<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Universal Links. Without this key an https link is just a web page and iOS never
	     offers it to us, which is the state the app was in while a perfectly good
	     apple-app-site-association file sat unused in web/ for months.

	     Both hosts are listed because fariin.com and www.fariin.com are different origins to
	     iOS, and a link pasted with the www is not rare.

	     THIS KEY CANNOT SHIP ALONE. The App ID com.kulan.messenger.native must have the
	     Associated Domains capability enabled in the Apple Developer portal, otherwise
	     get_provisioning_profile mints a profile without the entitlement and the build dies
	     at the signing step. Signing here is manual (see fastlane/Fastfile), so nothing
	     enables it for us. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:fariin.com</string>
		<string>applinks:www.fariin.com</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Lets AgeCheck.swift ask Apple for an age BAND (child / teen / adult) without ever asking
	     anybody for a birthday. Apple holds the real age on the Apple ID, and for children in a
	     Family Sharing group a parent set it, so this is the only real age signal available to us:
	     Google knows the age too and hands apps none of it, and email sign-up tells us nothing at
	     all. It works whichever door somebody signed up through, because the question goes to the
	     device rather than to the sign-in method.

	     SAME RULE AS THE KEY ABOVE, and the same failure it is documented against: the App ID
	     com.kulan.messenger.native must have the Declared Age Range capability enabled in the Apple
	     Developer portal, or get_provisioning_profile mints a profile without the entitlement and
	     the build dies at the signing step. ENABLED BY THE OWNER 2026-08-08, which is why this key
	     is here now and was deliberately held back when AgeCheck.swift landed in 8612386.

	     No second step was needed: fastlane runs get_provisioning_profile with force: true, so every
	     build mints a fresh profile and picks the new capability up on its own. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.declared-age-range</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
